  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>La Lanterne is one of the two beaches the City of Nice reserves for dogs. It sits at the western end of the Promenade des Anglais, behind the Carras harbour, not far from the airport: a pebble site slightly away from the centre, more than a postcard beach.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to nice.fr, Plage de la Lanterne (400 Promenade des Anglais) is open to dogs year-round, like Lenval beach. Note: the Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office lists it at 386 Promenade des Anglais; both sources refer to Plage de la Lanterne. The Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office specifies the rule by category: category 3 dogs may be off-leash and swim, category 2 dogs must be muzzled and kept on a leash, and category 1 dogs are banned. It is not the city's only dog beach: Lenval is the other.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: Plage de Lenval, closer to the centre, is Nice's other dog beach if you want a change of scenery.</div>

      <h2>The regulatory framework</h2>
      <p>The City of Nice links the opening of the two dog beaches to municipal order 2008-00481 and to article 26 of order 2010-2049. Everywhere else on the Nice coast, the tourist office specifies that dogs are not admitted on the beaches. La Lanterne is therefore not a seasonal tolerance: the City presents it as accessible all year, with no summer break.</p>
      <p>To place the site: Lenval beach, the other dog beach, is in the middle of the Promenade des Anglais, between numbers 137 and 147, and is recognisable in summer by two Totor statues and barriers. La Lanterne is at the far west, away from the centre, which explains its more discreet atmosphere. The neighbouring Carras and Forum beaches are supervised in summer but are not open to dogs.</p>

      <h2>In town with your dog</h2>
      <p>The City of Nice states that leashed dogs have access to all parks and gardens, except children's gardens and a few special parks (Monastère de Cimiez garden, villa Masséna garden, botanical garden, Phoenix park). Dog toilets (canisites) also exist in the city centre, notably at the Comte de Falicon garden, open 24/7. On the trams, all dogs are accepted free of charge, on a leash and muzzled; on the buses, only small dogs in a basket or carrier are admitted.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>In Saint-Laurent-du-Var, right next door, Vespins beach also accepts dogs — handy if the Lanterne is crowded.</p>
